SM(504,{"n":"Romans 3","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 3.mp3","d":3194.3,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.76,"So now chapter 3, verse 1."],[25.2,"What advantage then has the Jew?"],[28.0,"Or what is the prophet of circumcision?"],[30.42,"Well, this is the natural question."],[32.64,"So if we're no better off, in fact, if we're possibly worse off, for having had all of this extra knowledge, well then what what what does it mean?"],[42.09,"You know, we're God's chosen people."],[44.23,"You know, God loves Israel and chose them, you know, is it that that doesn't seem to make sense."],[52.31,"St. Paul answers that much in every way, chiefly because to them were committed the oracles of God."],[60.51,"So he says, big A number one."],[63.93,"Not just the Torah, not just, but all of the Hebrew Bible, the Old Testament, all of the oracles of God were given to you."],[73.29,"That's a very big benefit."],[76.01,"This is a very big benefit."],[79.85,"For what if some did not believe?"],[83.21,"Will their unbelief make the faithfulness of God without effect?"],[86.99,"So he says, sure, there are a whole bunch."],[90.81,"Who have not believed."],[92.17,"And he's referring to two things here."],[94.1,"One of them, again, is historical."],[97.16,"You go back through the Old Testament, you see the people who received the Torah and their track record."],[103.41,"Sort of the vast majority didn't really weren't faithful."],[107.51,"Where he talked about that word believe, it's about faithfulness and loyalty to God."],[111.19,"They weren't faithful, they didn't stay loyal to God, they didn't seek after God."],[115.09,"He's saying, does that invalidate God's faithfulness?"],[119.9,"Does that mean God somehow failed?"],[122.59,"Because all of these, this vast swath of his people turned against him."],[129.23,"So that's the first reference."],[130.91,"But he also has in mind what's going on right now."],[135.31,"Because what's going on right now?"],[137.39,"The vast majority of the Jewish people at this point have not accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[143.23,"So this isn't just a past thing"],[149.75,"This is a present thing."],[151.96,"So in this idea, has God that did God somehow fail?"],[155.32,"It's also has has Christ somehow failed now because most of his people did not receive him and rejected him."],[170.6,"Certainly not. Indeed, let God be true and every man a liar, as it is written, that you may be justified in your words and may overcome when you are judged."],[182.36,"That's a slightly different translation."],[184.6,"But at least you know where what psalm that's from, I hope."],[188.88,"Because you read it every week."],[191.04,"Yes, but I don't know its number."],[194.16,"It's it's uh in most of your English Bibles, it's Psalm 51."],[198.48,"It's Psalm 50 in the Greek numbering."],[201.68,"In the Orthodox Study Bible, in the Greek numbering."],[204.32,"It's Psalm 50."],[205.62,"Which is David's Psalm of Repentance."],[209.16,"It's David's Psalm of Repentance after committing murder, adultery."],[216.44,"Yeah. So uh after his sinfulness."],[220.16,"And do you remember the words that come right before that, or some approximation of them?"],[226.88,"Oh, I'm too on the spot to be vague."],[229.92,"The hidden truths of your wisdom. you have made clear to me"],[235.0,"Against you and you only have I sinned and done this evil in your sight."],[242.28,"That you may be justified in your words and prevail when you are judged."],[247.66,"So what is David saying there?"],[249.18,"David's saying, You gave me right all of your truth."],[253.98,"You revealed it to me."],[255.66,"Just like the Jewish people, who received the oracles of God, who St."],[259.44,"Paul's talking about."],[260.7,"He says, and yet, David says, I decided to sin against you."],[267.92,"And so uh this is, he's saying, This is one hundred percent my doing."],[273.76,"Well, this is an image of like a court case"],[275.12,"Say, when is God judged?"],[279.52,"And remember, court cases in the Old Testament in the Torah, you can look this up and double check me, were not like criminal cases."],[286.64,"They were like civil cases."],[289.52,"So it wasn't this person is accused of this crime, this is the penalty under the law."],[295.04,"There was no law code like that."],[297.84,"It was this person says this other person has wronged them."],[302.72,"And the judge attributes this party is the party in the right, this party is the party in the wrong."],[309.52,"So it's more between the two."],[310.92,"In this dispute."],[311.68,"So if we're judging between David and God."],[315.38,"God wins, God is in the right."],[317.14,"And David is completely in the wrong."],[317.3,"David is That's what saying."],[317.7,"completely He's saying, God, you are completely in the right, I am in the wrong."],[318.02,"And that's why St"],[325.26,"Paul quotes this here."],[327.82,"Because he's applying that to the whole nation."],[338.22,"And so that helps clarify that."],[341.68,"And so that's what that's that's what that is saying."],[343.6,"And Saint Paul is applying that to the whole nation of Israel."],[349.09,"Saying God revealed Israel, gave them Israel, gave them the Torah, gave them the rest."],[355.79,"And when they sinned, they were choosing to sin."],[358.19,"So they are completely in the wrong."],[359.71,"God is completely in the right."],[361.15,"He gave everything to them and they made their decision"],[361.51,"God by no means failed."],[371.91,"But if our unrighteousness demonstrates the righteousness of God, what shall we say?"],[380.15,"So David says this."],[382.47,"Well, see, God, God's in the right."],[385.79,"So, you know, us when we sin, when we fall."],[391.05,"It helps us realize how great and faithful and true God is."],[397.19,"So St. Paul says, W,ell is that what we're saying?"],[400.09,"Is that what we're saying?"],[400.89,"That the sinfulness of man just shows how great and glorious God is."],[407.25,"And if so."],[408.95,"Is God unjust who inflicts wrath?"],[412.08,"Well, then why is God upset about sin then?"],[414.82,"It brings glory to him."],[418.26,"That's pretty sophist."],[421.14,"You say it's sophist."],[423.46,"You say it's sophist."],[427.22,"Our Calvinist friends assert exactly this."],[435.06,"They say exactly this, that God creates some people to be sinful and wicked, to condemn them to hell for his glory. so that he can reveal his wrath and his justice to human beings"],[453.34,"Okay? So it may sound like sophistry, but there are still people among us who believe this."],[480.06,"But as someone might say."],[482.2,"Some person might try to make this argument in the 16th century."],[487.84,"Certainly not. For then, how will God judge the world?"],[494.7,"How will God judge the world?"],[497.5,"If everything brings him glory."],[501.02,"If everything just magnifies it, regardless of order, then how is he going to restore justice and order?"],[510.39,"By saying that, you're saying that everything is already in good order."],[515.83,"Everything is already the way that God wants it."],[519.19,"God just happens to be."],[521.07,"He wants this sin to be there so that he could display his wrath."],[526.11,"He wants other righteousness to exist so he can display his mercy."],[533.63,"That's the basic Calvinist idea."],[535.95,"God is all powerful, and so everything is according to it."],[540.03,"God is the only agent."],[542.27,"Everything that happens is God's doing and his plan, which means there is not a situation on this earth to be corrected on the day of the Lord."],[552.19,"Everything is always and already the way, exactly the way God wants it to be."],[560.43,"And they will go so far as to say that it's sinful for us to then question that."],[565.72,"For us to dare to question that."],[567.88,"But St. Paul is saying that's ridiculous."],[570.76,"Isn't there a muscle?"],[572.12,"The very idea, the very idea that God is going to come and judge the world."],[576.2,"What judgment means."],[577.1,"Remember Mishpat. The whole concept of judgment in the Hebrew scriptures is that God is going to set things right, to put them back in order the way they are supposed to be."],[589.38,"Which means they're not the way they're supposed to be now."],[592.74,"They're not the way he wants them to be now."],[596.32,"But he's allowing that, as St."],[598.48,"Paul has already said, and he's going to talk more about this later."],[601.2,"God is allowing things to remain out of order for now."],[607.44,"Why did St."],[608.4,"Paul say that was?"],[610.0,"To give time in his patience and in his mercy to give time for repentance."],[616.0,"So that the wicked will have time to repent."],[619.2,"Because as he says in Isaiah, God takes no delight in the death of the wicked man."],[628.52,"It says God desires that none should perish, but that they should turn and live."],[636.2,"And so he bears with them in his patience."],[638.28,"That's what St."],[638.84,"Paul's been arguing here along."],[640.68,"And so what St."],[641.64,"Paul is saying here is the very idea that God is going to come and judge the earth, if we understand what judgment means, that it's not some kind of criminal trial."],[651.22,"That it's God restoring correct order."],[654.5,"The fact that he's going to do that means things are out of whack now."],[658.98,"And so you can't use, well, God wants me to sin so he can display his righte.ousness as an excuse"],[687.66,"So he's continuing the same idea."],[689.76,"So I go and lie."],[692.41,"And based on my lying, you come to understand God's truth."],[698.03,"In contrast. And God wanted me to lie."],[700.09,"And God wanted me to lie to display this."],[702.75,"To bring glory to him."],[704.81,"To bring glory to him."],[705.77,"So then why would you judge me to be a sinner."],[709.94,"I'm doing what God created me to do."],[714.24,"I'm bringing glory to God by being who I am and doing what I am."],[720.88,"And why not say, Let us do evil that good may come, then?"],[728.29,"As we are slanderously reported, and as some affirm that we say, their condemnation is just."],[735.27,"So he says, which some people are saying that that's what we're saying."],[739.09,"Because remember what the the slander against Saint Paul was in axe."],[743.25,"He's telling everybody not to worry about the law."],[747.46,"He's telling everybody it doesn't matter what you do."],[750.46,"Just go and just go and sit."],[753.06,"And he, of course, is saying not only"],[759.07,"Their condemnation is just kind of soft pedals."],[764.35,"Soft pedals what he's saying."],[766.75,"So people will have to minorly pardon my French."],[769.39,"He's basically saying they can go to hell."],[777.71,"That's the kind of condemnation we're talking about."],[781.05,"Because he's just been talking about Christ judging the earth."],[784.65,"Condemnation is the right."],[788.91,"And so they're claiming that this is what St."],[792.75,"Paul is teaching."],[795.79,"He goes on, what then?"],[798.55,"Are we better than they?"],[801.99,"The people who are we better than those people who I just said could go to final condemnation."],[810.29,"Am I saying that because I think I'm better than them?"],[813.49,"And you who I'm writing to are better than them."],[817.81,"He says, not at all."],[819.25,"If we have previously charged both Jews and Greeks, and Greeks is here doesn't just mean Greek people."],[826.69,"It means it's referring to Gentiles."],[830.69,"That they are all under sin."],[835.17,"That phrase is interesting too."],[838.05,"Notice he doesn't say that they have all committed sins."],[840.69,"Paul, we gloss over it very quickly"],[843.29,"You've got to watch what because St."],[847.93,"Saint Paul will talk about sins, and he will talk about sin."],[854.89,"And this is these are two different ways."],[857.21,"A lot of times when he talks about sins, a lot of English translations will translate it as trespasses."],[865.21,"Because sins, trespasses, the idea there is that there's a law, there's a commandment, and you're breaking it."],[874.57,"He's talking about sin."],[877.13,"Sin is first talked about that way, way back in Genesis, when uh Cain and Abel bring their offerings to God. and Abel's is accepted and Cain's isn't, and Cain's mad"],[891.57,"And God comes to Cain and he says, Sin is crouching at your door."],[897.25,"He talks about like it's an animal."],[898.77,"That crouching is like it's ready to pounce."],[902.25,"It seeks to master you."],[904.81,"You need to master it."],[908.25,"It's gonna come and try and take control."],[911.85,"You need to fight."],[914.19,"You need to fight back."],[915.15,"Of course, we know Cain doesn't."],[916.99,"He gives in to his hatred and he goes and he murders his brother."],[921.39,"But this is the concept of sin that St."],[925.31,"Paul's talking about."],[926.11,"Remember, he said he talked about being obedient to unrighteousness."],[930.69,"So it's not just, well, whether you're a Jew or a Greek, at some point in your life you've messed up and made a mistake."],[937.67,"Or at some point in your life you've committed a sin."],[940.23,"At some point in your life, you're disobedient."],[943.03,"He's saying everyone has now come under the power of the power of sin."],[951.83,"This is the problem."],[953.59,"He's saying all of us have a common problem."],[957.59,"Remember, he's talking about the unity of the church in Rome."],[961.03,"Whether you're a Jew, whether you're a Gentile, we all face this common problem."],[966.23,"It's not just the Gentiles who are out there idolatrous, no good sinners, and then we Jews were, you know, good and holy and following God."],[975.97,"No, both. Alright, all are under the power of sin."],[981.09,"And so then he quotes from a whole series of psalms."],[984.61,"These are all psalm verses."],[986.69,"This is not one continuous psalm."],[991.33,"And he even throws in a little Ecclesiastes there at one point."],[994.53,"But uh what seems like a reference to Ecclesiastes."],[997.73,"He says, as it is written, just a note on that phrase, because I think it's been a while since I I talked about it."],[1006.13,"You know, we we see that in the New Testament, we think, oh, that's just what you do before you quote scripture."],[1011.89,"But some of the force of the phrase as it is written is lost on us because we write things all the time."],[1017.81,"We jot notes, we type things into our phone, we type tablets"],[1023.13,"Remember, at this time in history, writing something down is a big deal."],[1030.7,"We talked about how getting a copy of a book this length of Romans would cost the equivalent of about $5,000 to get that copy made."],[1040.94,"Right in today's money."],[1043.26,"Okay. So there was no printing press."],[1046.96,"You couldn't just jot."],[1049.2,"No, there were no photocopiers."],[1051.89,"So this was a major thing."],[1054.05,"So uh now we we do have the equivalent of scrap paper in the ancient world."],[1058.85,"It's mostly pottery shards."],[1060.53,"Like scratch. notes into pottery shards and things"],[1063.21,"But for something to be written in a written document like a book, it had to be very important."],[1070.01,"So as it is written, the fact that, you know, the word that we translate as scriptures, well, transliterate, because that's really from a Latin word."],[1079.4,"Is the word just things that are written down."],[1084.92,"Because writing something down like this sort of enshrined it and made it permanent."],[1091.17,"As opposed to all the other knowledge and things that we pass on by word of mouth through storytelling and oral tradition in our communities."],[1098.55,"That we all teach each other."],[1100.07,"Certain things are written down because they're seem to be especially important, and we need to preserve this just the way it is."],[1104.31,"Okay So whatever whatever scripture is quoted, and it's not always quoted this way, but whatever it's quoted is as it is written."],[1114.96,"That's an appeal to its authority."],[1118.53,"This was written down and enshrined, for all time."],[1124.15,"So this is this is important and true."],[1129.27,"And as I said, it's a whole series of these psalm verses."],[1131.83,"There's none righteous, no not one."],[1133.83,"There is none who understands."],[1135.23,"There is none who seeks after God."],[1137.27,"They have all turned aside."],[1138.95,"They have together become unprofitable."],[1141.27,"There is none who does good, no not one."],[1144.23,"Their throat is an open tomb, with their tongues, they have practiced deceit."],[1148.03,"The poison of asps is under their lips, whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness, their feet are swift to shed blood, destruction and misery are in their ways, and the way of peace they have not known."],[1159.31,"There is no fear of God before their eyes."],[1163.39,"Now he's presenting this."],[1178.07,"He's not saying these are the crimes I charge each and every one of you with."],[1183.44,"He's describing a state of being."],[1186.64,"He's describing what it is like to be under the power of sin."],[1191.6,"This is what life is like when you are under the control of sin."],[1196.32,"And this is a horrible state to be in."],[1200.88,"So when he talks about, as he's about to, the solution to this problem."],[1206.61,"He's not talking about here's how you get off the hook for the guilt of these horrible things you've done."],[1214.05,"He's talking about here's how you escape."],[1217.41,"Here's how you are set free from this sort of life under the power of sin."],[1226.69,"This is how you become free to seek after good, to seek after God."],[1230.53,"How you become free of you know this poison that's in your mouth. and how you become free of this of the violence, all the sins he described in chapter one"],[1243.45,"These are things that have taken control of you, that have taken possession of you."],[1246.25,"You're living under them."],[1249.29,"This is an oppression that's upon you."],[1251.21,"This is something horrible that's happened to you."],[1253.13,"This is what you seek salvation from."],[1255.71,"From this state."],[1257.63,"And condemnation, then, if salvation is being saved from this state, then condemnation is remaining in this state forever."],[1267.82,"Because you choose to remain in this state."],[1274.46,"So this is this is also a picture of what condemnation is, of what wrath is, of what it is to live in that way."],[1288.18,"Now we know that whoever the law, I'm sorry, that whatever the law says, it says to those who are under the law, that every mouth may be stopped, and all the world may become guilty before God."],[1300.58,"Therefore, by the deeds of the law, no flesh will be justified in his sight."],[1305.54,"For by the law is the knowledge of sin."],[1315.86,"This gets into what the purpose, St."],[1320.18,"Paul believes the Torah serves is."],[1324.56,"And it's not, as he says here, no one is made righteous by the law."],[1330.4,"Or by keeping it."],[1332.64,"What does it mean made righteous?"],[1334.64,"That means that's not how you get to the right side on the judgment."],[1341.12,"There's people on the right and the left, sheep goats."],[1343.62,"You don't become a sheep by keeping the law."],[1345.62,"That's not what it's for."],[1348.24,"That's not what it's for."],[1350.64,"What he says is that by the law is the knowledge of sin."],[1358.42,"So the through the law, everyone's mouth is shut."],[1363.46,"And everyone, everyone stands."],[1366.1,"I don't know the that the word guilty is the best, but everyone stands on condemned side."],[1371.35,"But it's before God."],[1373.91,"Because the law reveals that to you."],[1377.73,"The law reveals that to you."],[1379.89,"And the law, as we mentioned before, contains a means for managing that."],[1386.15,"It contains a means for managing that."],[1388.87,"This is what happens on the Day of Atonement."],[1392.6,"God has come to live in the midst of his people."],[1397.0,"So when they're in the wilderness, they have this camp."],[1399.24,"The tabernacle is right in the middle of it."],[1402.28,"God is living there, he's dwelling there in the midst of his people."],[1406.52,"Here's the problem."],[1408.04,"God is holy."],[1408.84,"God's holiness is a consuming fire"],[1414.96,"The people he's living in the midst of aren't so holy."],[1420.56,"They're not very holy."],[1422.24,"And so the phrase that's used usually in English translations is the threat is that God will break out and like out of the tabernacle and destroy them."],[1433.2,"They'll be consumed with their sins."],[1436.32,"So either that'll happen or God will have to leave."],[1441.44,"He'll have to leave because of their sinfulness."],[1443.92,"And so the Day of Atonement ritual that's practiced every year"],[1448.76,"And we'll talk more about this in Hebrews because it goes into more detail."],[1452.16,"But the Day of Atonement ritual is practiced every year."],[1453.68,"No sheep They take two goats."],[1455.8,"Okay. So when Christ is called the Lamb of God, it's not talking about atonement."],[1461.56,"It's talking about Passover, but that's a side note."],[1464.36,"It takes two goats."],[1466.04,"Okay. One of those goats, the priest, the high priest comes and places all of the sins of the people on that goat."],[1475.8,"That goat is then not killed."],[1477.96,"That goat is not sacrificed."],[1479.88,"You can't sacrifice that goat because now it's unclean because it's got all the sins of the people on it."],[1484.84,"It's bearing all the sins of the people."],[1486.92,"It can't be a sacrifice now."],[1489.8,"That goat's driven out into the wilderness to take those sins out into the desert."],[1496.94,"Away from the people, out of the camp."],[1497.82,"Because it's the goat that's sent out"],[1500.1,"Yeah. That's why it's referred to as the scapegoat."],[1505.09,"Gets sent out into the wilderness."],[1507.01,"The second goat that does not have the sins on it gets sacrificed."],[1512.05,"And its blood gets taken into the most holy place."],[1514.77,"And the blood is used."],[1516.93,"The word uh if the verb is kephir, it in the noun is kippur."],[1522.53,"That's where we get Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement."],[1525.25,"Uh the word means to wipe."],[1528.79,"To wipe. And the idea is that the sins are now gone, but the sins have left a stain on the camp."],[1538.31,"The things the people have done."],[1539.93,"Just as God says to Adam, cursed is the ground because of you."],[1545.27,"And uh as God says in Leviticus, certain sins will taint the land, so that the land will vomit you out to get rid of you because of the abominations you're committing."],[1556.48,"There's this taint now in the camp, this uncleanness."],[1560.21,"That also has to be purified."],[1562.77,"It has to be washed away."],[1566.13,"So there's those two elements."],[1570.1,"Sins are taken away, sin is taken away, and the damage, is uh is purified and cleaned."],[1580.01,"So what does this do?"],[1581.37,"This manages the sins of the people."],[1581.85,"manages But it the situation."],[1585.49,"Doesn't get rid of it but manages it."],[1589.82,"They're still going to keep sinning."],[1591.1,"We have to do this every year."],[1594.3,"And the same thing with the sin offerings."],[1596.88,"The same thing with the other in this cot."],[1598.96,"Every day we're having to do the sin offerings."],[1602.42,"And the priest has to go in and offer it for his own sins and for the people's sins, because he's sinning too."],[1608.63,"So it's just this management system."],[1612.23,"This management that reveals sin and then manages it."],[1617.16,"Until what? Until Christ."],[1621.24,"Until it's dealt with."],[1625.25,"So God adds this, the Torah as this sin management system for his people."],[1631.57,"So he can continue to dwell with them and be in their presence."],[1635.81,"Until the time comes when he comes to dwell with them in the person of Jesus Christ and then takes care of it once and for all."],[1647.01,"But that means it was never salvation."],[1652.69,"It's Christ through his death and resurrection, ascension into heaven, that removes us from being under sin."],[1660.45,"Who frees us from sin?"],[1662.21,"Who frees us from this state."],[1664.91,"Who moves us over into the righteous column from the condemned column."],[1670.71,"We become sheep."],[1671.95,"Sheep from goats, yeah."],[1675.31,"The Torah never claimed to do that."],[1679.87,"It never said it was going to do that."],[1683.47,"It was never meant to do that."],[1687.47,"And so when St."],[1689.31,"Paul says that no one is justified by the Torah, it's not because no one ever kept it perfectly."],[1696.35,"It's because even if you keep it perfectly, because remember, it includes the sacrifices."],[1701.23,"You don't have to never sin to keep it perfectly."],[1705.28,"In fact, God says, when God comes to Jeroboam."],[1711.05,"When the k theing kingdom is divided, into the northern kingdom and the southern kingdom, and Jeroboam, son of Nebat, becomes the first king of northerndom."],[1718.69,"He gets ten tribes."],[1719.89,"God comes to him and says, I made a covenant with David that a uh son of his body, a son of his flesh, a son of his line, one of his descendants, would sit on his throne forever."],[1732.15,"He says, I will make the same covenant with you, Jeroboam, if you like David, keep every one of my laws and follow every one of my commandments."],[1744.55,"Was God not aware of Psalm 50 that we were just talking about?"],[1749.76,"No. Because David repented."],[1754.48,"David repented. David followed the means that God had set for managing his sin after he had committed it."],[1765.46,"Through repentance. And so he had kept the Torah."],[1770.42,"St. Paul at another point is going to say that he's blameless in terms of the Torah."],[1777.62,"He's going to say that was true back when he was a Pharisee before Jesus."],[1783.14,"He's not being facetious."],[1786.4,"He followed it."],[1787.28,"But the point is that doesn't get you."],[1791.89,"That manages your sin."],[1794.37,"It's a band-aid."],[1795.33,"It doesn't set you free from sin."],[1797.51,"It's like if you go to a doc doctortor. and the, rather than curing the disease, just sort of manages your symptoms"],[1804.17,"Well, I'll give you something that'll take away the fever, and I'll give you something that'll take away the headache."],[1808.97,"You're still going to have a sinus infection, but you know, you won't notice so much."],[1814.59,"That's not dealing with the problem."],[1816.03,"It may make you able to continue to function while you have a sinus infection, but it's not curing the problem."],[1824.19,"It's Christ who comes and cures the problem."],[1827.57,"And Christ was the plan all along."],[1830.13,"So until Christ, we have this sin management system to allow God to dwell among his people during that time."],[1841.41,"To prepare that people to produce Christ"],[1847.66,"Because Christ comes from that people."],[1853.5,"And so that's background now to what St."],[1855.74,"Paul is about to say."],[1857.66,"He says, but now."],[1859.76,"As before with the law, that's what the law did."],[1861.84,"That's what the Torah did."],[1863.92,"But now the righteousness of God, apart from the law, is revealed, being witnessed by the law and the prophets."],[1873.04,"So what God has done in Christ isn't part of the law."],[1877.68,"But the law and the prophets bore witness to it, spoke to it."],[1883.28,"It's the fulfillment of what. the law and the prophets were aiming at"],[1889.8,"Even the righteousness of God through faith in Jesus Christ to all and on all who believe."],[1899.4,"So this is the beginning of him arguing."],[1902.54,"I just argued everyone has the same problem being under sin."],[1906.7,"Everyone has the same solution."],[1911.18,"Whether you're a Jew or a Greek, the solution to being righteous before God, being on the right side at the judgment, is in Jesus Christ and found through belief, remember, which is faith, which is faithfulness, loyalty, to Christ."],[1932.44,"Which includes, of course, believing that the gospel that's proclaimed to you is true."],[1938.17,"But then goes beyond that, beyond just saying, okay, yeah."],[1942.17,"True, false. For there is no difference."],[1950.29,"Again, no difference between Jews and Greeks."],[1955.09,"For all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, being justified, being made righteous, freely by his grace, through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus."],[1967.65,"Now there's a whole bunch of stuff packed into there."],[1970.61,"And St. Paul's gonna spend a lot of the rest of Romans sort of on we've you've probably noticed that already."],[1976.25,"Like when we started chapter one, there was a whole bunch of stuff packed in, and then he sort of unpacked it."],[1981.05,"We're getting the same thing here now in chapter three."],[1983.55,"A whole bunch of stuff packed into what he just said, and now he's gonna kind of unpack it."],[1990.51,"But so uh all, meaning Jews, Gentiles, everybody, have sinned."],[1996.67,"Now notice this is not just we talked about the difference."],[2001.31,"Under sin, this is the deep have sinned."],[2005.21,"Everyone has committed sins, all have fallen short of the glory of God."],[2009.43,"Which again implies the glory of God does not come from people sinning."],[2012.79,"But that's a slight digression."],[2016.21,"Being justified freely by his grace, right"],[2023.13,"Meaning, what is grace?"],[2027.85,"Grace is the action of God."],[2033.21,"That's what grace means."],[2035.29,"It means God does it."],[2037.5,"You're justified by the action of God."],[2044.43,"So by the grace of God means. of the action"],[2047.63,"That God did it."],[2048.57,"Yeah. Through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus."],[2053.77,"Okay. What is redemption?"],[2056.65,"What does it mean when you redeem a coupon?"],[2062.95,"Redemption, the idea of redemption is related to the idea of slavery."],[2069.73,"It's related to the idea of manumission."],[2072.82,"If you're a slave, you belong to someone or something."],[2076.66,"In this case, we're talking about sin."],[2078.36,"Because St. Paul said everyone is under sin."],[2080.76,"You're a slave to sin."],[2082.2,"Christ has redeemed you."],[2083.64,"He's purchased you out of slavery."],[2086.44,"So remember, in the ancient world, you became a slave because you had a debt you couldn't pay."],[2091.02,"So you're going to go work it off, basically."],[2093.64,"Well, if someone came and paid that debt, you're now free."],[2098.41,"'re now free. You"],[2104.34,"So everyone, by sinning, came to be under sin."],[2110.18,"Came to be enslaved by sin."],[2112.42,"You start out making choices."],[2114.02,"After a while, you're not making choices anymore."],[2117.0,"When you're sinning."],[2118.68,"I talk about in terms of addiction in the modern world, I think is a good example."],[2124.2,"The first few times you take the drink or do the drug, you're choosing to do it."],[2129.48,"After a while, you're not choosing anymore."],[2132.54,"Because it's controlling you."],[2134.62,"All sins are that way."],[2136.47,"You may decide to lose your cool and get angry the first couple times, but after a while the anger's running you."],[2142.47,"You're not choosing to get angry anymore."],[2148.77,"So sin come under sin."],[2151.62,"But we become righteous."],[2154.28,"Not through keeping Torah."],[2158.04,"We can become righteous through the action of God, who in Jesus Christ has redeemed us, who has bought us back, who has redeemed us from that slavery."],[2176.36,"Oh, here we go."],[2178.68,"Whom God set forth in the this is one of those places where they didn't change the new King James."],[2183.36,"Whom God set forth as a propitiation by his blood through faith to demonstrate his righteousness."],[2191.44,"Because in his forbearance, God had passed over the sins that were previously committed."],[2196.96,"Okay. So the word there that's translated propitiation is a Greek word, Elisterium."],[2204.48,"Okay. Which is the word that's used at the Old Testament for the cover of the Ark of the Covenant."],[2212.24,"Sometimes translated in English as the mercy seat."],[2215.93,"But that is what was purified on the day of atonement."],[2220.65,"Okay. So this is a reference to atonement."],[2227.21,"Rather than propitiation, atoning sacrifice would probably be a better translation."],[2231.45,"Paul is doing is he's paralleling what Christ did to the Day of Atonement"],[2231.65,"Because what St."],[2239.89,"But propitiation bothers me because it sounds like God's anger has to be appeased."],[2250.54,"Which is the pagan use."],[2254.38,"Yeah, I guess I'll go into it."],[2256.06,"Okay. So the the, and this is the way a number of R, again, those who follow Martin Luther, those who follow John Calvin, uh the way they take this."],[2268.86,"Rather than basing their interpretation of this word on the way it's used in the Greek Old Testament, and the Hebrew words that it's related to, okay, they choose to base it on the pagan use."],[2286.16,"And the pagan use of this word is for a sacrifice, basically, when a god is angry at you."],[2292.96,"You've done something, and you didn't have to do much in the pagan world."],[2296.24,"You've done something to upset."],[2298.82,"Some pagan god or spirit or other, and so you go and you offer it the blood of some animal, in order to appease it and get it to lay off and not kill you."],[2310.32,"Basically, and not destroy you."],[2312.44,"Okay. There are a bunch of problems with that."],[2316.6,"Uh trying to import that here."],[2318.6,"But let me tell you one of the most obvious ones"],[2321.68,"What has Saint Paul just quoted?"],[2324.24,"What psalm has he just quoted?"],[2326.24,"He's just the 50s."],[2328.32,"Psalm 50. How does that psalm end?"],[2335.6,"With David saying you do not delight in burnt offerings."],[2341.2,"In burnt offerings and whole burnt offerings."],[2343.52,"Sacrifice. Yeah. But a contrite and humble heart you will not despise."],[2350.72,"Okay. So the psalm that St."],[2355.6,"Paul has just quoted says that God doesn't work that way."],[2362.24,"So how would St."],[2363.28,"Paul quote that? psalm and then turn around"],[2367.96,"And all along here, St."],[2369.16,"Paul's quoting the Old Testament."],[2371.16,"But now when he uses this word, he's not referring to the Old Testament anymore."],[2375.88,"He's comparing God to Zeus."],[2383.32,"It doesn't make sense."],[2385.8,"It just doesn't make sense."],[2387.56,"It's very clear Saint Paul's referring to the Day of Atonement."],[2392.73,"That he offered Christ as an atoning sacrifice."],[2404.41,"So what does that mean?"],[2406.89,"That means Christ's blood."],[2407.93,"Washes away the taint that's left"],[2413.87,"By, remember, this was sins, have sinned."],[2417.81,"The taint that's left by those sins."],[2420.05,"It's gone completely, permanently."],[2425.73,"So it's not just that we're deposited over in this righteous category."],[2431.51,"God picks us up from over here and drops us off over here, but there's no change."],[2436.87,"This is again contra our Lutheran and Calvinist friends."],[2441.29,"It's not that God just changes the category he looks at us in."],[2445.34,"But that we're actually purified by Christ's blood."],[2450.64,"We're actually purified by Christ's blood."],[2455.12,"And the word justify, the words translated here justified, actually contains that idea."],[2464.56,"It's it the Aramaic word that it's covering."],[2470.1,"The Aramaic word is used in Daniel."],[2473.14,"Daniel talks about how after the time that the temple has been trampled by the Gentiles, referring to Antiochus Epiphanes."],[2480.74,"R rightight? The Maccabean revolt."],[2482.98,"When that time is over, the temple will be justified."],[2489.14,"What does that mean?"],[2490.26,"The temple will have its sins forgiven."],[2494.38,"The temple will have the reputed righteousness of Christ applied to it?"],[2499.98,"The desecration will be undone."],[2502.32,"It will be cleansed."],[2503.76,"It'll be purified."],[2505.36,"That's what happens."],[2506.32,"That's what Hanukkah celebrates."],[2508.58,"It'll be purified."],[2511.46,"Again. So this idea of purification is contained in the word justified, too."],[2516.58,"But just in case, you're not going to pick up on that because the Greek doesn't necessarily contain that idea."],[2522.39,"St. Paul adds this imagery of the mercy seat, the cover of the Ark of the Covenant."],[2528.15,"Where on the day of atonement, the high priest goes in and takes the blood and wipes it."],[2535.96,"To purify. the altar or the uh the cover"],[2546.96,"So again, this is going to be further unpacked."],[2549.78,"But he's saying Christ is the means."],[2552.2,"Whether you're a Jew or a Gentile, that this is taken care of, that we're not only moved over to this category, but that we're purified of our sins."],[2562.31,"And that we're then by being purified of our sins, we're no longer under sin."],[2567.91,"We're no longer living in the way those psalm verses described."],[2571.69,"And so now we can seek after God."],[2575.39,"We can do these things that then God rewards with eternal life."],[2579.71,"So God gives it to us in the first place and then he rewards it"],[2587.27,"Right. Okay. And he does this."],[2596.23,"Why? It says to demonstrate his righteousness."],[2599.53,"To demonstrate God's justice."],[2602.33,"Because in the past, as we mentioned, he passed over sins."],[2609.05,"He's been showing this patience and this forbearance."],[2612.89,"He's been showing it with the Gentiles."],[2615.79,"And not punishing their sins."],[2618.59,"He's been showing it really with Israel, too, by giving them the Torah to sort of manage."],[2623.23,"Manage the sin problem all this time"],[2629.64,"God's been doing that for X thousands of years."],[2634.12,"And St. Paul says now he displays his justice, that he is a just God, because he deals with the problem."],[2643.56,"He solves the problem."],[2646.12,"He's not just bearing with it anymore, but he solves it."],[2657.4,"To demonstrate at the present time his righteousness, that he might be just and the justifier of the one who has faith in Jesus."],[2667.61,"So this shows that he's just right and that he deals with the problem."],[2676.57,"And he deals with it by justifying, by making righteous."],[2682.06,"Those who have faith."],[2685.8,"Jesus, who are faithful, who are loyal to Christ."],[2688.7,"Who believe and who follow Christ, he justifies them."],[2692.06,"And that this displays his justice."],[2693.82,"He solves the problem by purifying, forgiving, cleansing."],[2701.58,"Those who believe in Christ."],[2706.38,"Where is boasting then?"],[2707.98,"It is excluded."],[2711.38,"So can anybody come and brag about the fact that anything."],[2721.46,"No. Because we were all under sin."],[2723.3,"We were all enslaved."],[2725.22,"And Christ came and set us free."],[2727.38,"Any more than the Israelites on their way out of Egypt could brag."],[2731.14,"They didn't do anything."],[2733.86,"God doesn't come to the Israelites in Egypt and give them the Torah."],[2740.1,"And say, when you guys follow this perfectly."],[2743.87,"When you guys become really righteous, I'm going to set you free and give you a land."],[2750.03,"He comes, they didn't do anything"],[2750.95,"No. The exact opposite."],[2757.84,"He comes, redeems them, so he brings them out, and then he gives them the Torah for this other for this other purpose."],[2765.36,"Deuteronomy 9. He says to them in the wilderness, do not think that you are that it is because of your righteousness that I've brought you out of Egypt and brought you here."],[2777.22,"For you are a stiff-necked people."],[2784.42,"What is boasting then is excluded by what law of works?"],[2788.34,"No but by the law of faith."],[2791.38,"I'm going to continue because we have to understand what's being said here"],[2794.78,"Therefore, we conclude that a man is justified by faith apart from the deeds of the law."],[2802.78,"That's important here, because he's not just talking about works in general."],[2808.08,"We already saw Christ is going to judge everyone according to his works."],[2818.56,"So he's talking about the works of the law, the deeds of the law."],[2824.32,"By keeping the law, that's not how you get from one category to the other."],[2832.73,"And what are the what would the works of the law be in particular in terms of his audience now."],[2842.21,"Circumcision. Uh-huh, for example"],[2843.37,"What are the things that they would be arguing the Gentiles should do?"],[2849.05,"Circumcision, keeping kosher."],[2853.51,"Terms of the food laws."],[2856.44,"Etc. etc. What do all those things have in common?"],[2862.44,"All of those things, remember when we talked about Acts 15?"],[2866.92,"And we talked about how there were only certain commandments that were applied to the Gentiles?"],[2871.0,"That that went back to Leviticus."],[2873.08,"That in Leviticus, out of the whole code, there's only a couple of things that it says this applies to you and to any Gentile you have living among you."],[2882.44,"So these are things."],[2883.52,"And it wasn't the food laws, it wasn't circumcision."],[2887.5,"It wasn't any of these things."],[2888.94,"Those commandments were only given to the people of Israel."],[2892.62,"And God never said, I want you to go out and enforce these rules on the other nations."],[2899.4,"I want you to go out and, you know, contra Islam."],[2901.88,"I don't want you to go out and force, you know, the uh the Hittites to keep kosher."],[2908.53,"I don't want you to go out and force uh the the uh the Hittites to be circumcised."],[2917.83,"He never said that."],[2919.29,"He didn't he didn't give those laws to them."],[2921.13,"And in fact, uh, you look at uh Naaman the leper, who's a Syrian."],[2927.63,"He goes back to Syria"],[2928.23,"He comes, he's cleansed, he says, From now on I worship only Yahweh."],[2933.91,"He's not told, oh, by the way, you need to be circumcised, and you need to keep kosher, and you need to celebrate the Passover, and you need to."],[2945.4,"None of that."],[2946.2,"It's you worship no other God but Yahweh and then sexual immorality."],[2950.92,"Morality. And you follow God and live a moral life."],[2955.72,"So these works of the law he's talking about were part of this sin management system."],[2964.53,"And part of their identity as the people of Israel."],[2968.21,"They have nothing to do with, he just argued circumcision in particular, has nothing to do with how you're judged on the last day, whether you're one of the righteous or one of the condemned."],[2980.25,"If you're uncircumcised and righteous, you're on the righteous side."],[2984.97,"It's perfectly possible to be circumcised and condemned."],[2988.09,"It's perfectly possible to never eat pork, but to be wicked and be condemned."],[2993.77,"That was never the purpose of these things."],[3015.21,"So it's not by doing those. things that you're the but it's by faith"],[3021.39,"By faith, by faithfulness, by loyalty to Christ."],[3026.49,"Now he's gonna go on."],[3027.53,"We're not gonna get into chapter four tonight, but in chapter four, he's gonna go on and argue by the way, it was always that way."],[3033.47,"The law was never about that."],[3034.59,"It's not that something changed."],[3036.45,"Like the law was the way to do that."],[3038.37,"The Torah was about that, but now it's not."],[3041.25,"No, it's the Torah was never about that."],[3043.09,"It was always, because he's gonna go back to Abraham."],[3046.61,"So it was always about faithfulness, loyalty."],[3050.62,"Believing God's promises."],[3052.62,"That's always what it was about in terms of being on that righteous side."],[3058.3,"And the Torah was. added later for this other purpose"],[3063.3,"But that's where that's where he's gonna go ultimately."],[3068.82,"So he says, or is he the God of the Jews only?"],[3073.22,"Is he not also the God of the Gentiles?"],[3075.94,"Yes, of the Gentiles also."],[3078.48,"So this is all right."],[3079.78,"He's only the God of the Jews?"],[3081.54,"Are there some other gods?"],[3084.72,"To his Jewish audience."],[3085.92,"Well, no, he's the he's God."],[3088.5,"He created the heavens and the earth."],[3089.94,"He created all the nations."],[3091.43,"He scattered them at the Tower of Babel."],[3093.93,"But he's the God of the whole universe."],[3096.47,"The things they worship aren't gods."],[3104.11,"Since there is one God who will justify the circumcised by faith and the uncircumcised through faith."],[3112.25,"So God doesn't justify the Jews through circumcision."],[3117.15,"And the uncircumcised by some other means."],[3119.79,"Circumcision has nothing to do with it."],[3124.67,"Do we then make void the law through faith?"],[3127.79,"Certainly not. On the contrary, we establish the law."],[3131.31,"So he's saying, what are we saying?"],[3132.75,"We're saying the Torah is void."],[3134.56,"It's abolished, it's done."],[3136.82,"And he's going to expand on this more, but what he's saying is, no."],[3140.18,"We establish it."],[3141.72,"We establish it because we make its true purpose. clear"],[3147.6,"We make its true purpose clear so that it can serve the function it was intended to serve."],[3155.52,"Which wasn't to make people righteous."],[3160.8,"It never was."],[3165.52,"So we went longer than I intended to, but we'll go ahead and stop here."],[3170.24,"Thank you, everybody."],[3172.0,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[3179.28,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3184.64,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.76,"lv":2,"h":"3:1-2 - The Jew's advantage","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:1</b> - \"What advantage then has the Jew? Or what is the profit of circumcision?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">natural question: if we're no better off, <b>possibly worse off</b> for the extra knowledge, what does it mean?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God's chosen people... God loves Israel and chose them\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:2</b> - \"much in every way, chiefly\" - <b>oracles of God</b> committed to them</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's \"<b>big A, number one</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">not just Torah - <b>all of the Hebrew Bible</b>, the OT</li><li class=\"d1\">\"a very big benefit\"</li></ul>"},{"t":79.85,"lv":2,"h":"3:3-4 - Unbelief and God's faithfulness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:3</b> - if some did not believe, does their unbelief nullify the <b>faithfulness of God</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Two references</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>historical</b>: OT track record - vast majority of those who received Torah <b>weren't faithful</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"believe\" = <b>faithfulness and loyalty</b> to God; didn't seek after God</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>present</b>: most of the Jewish people <b>haven't accepted Jesus as Messiah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Did God fail? Has <b>Christ</b> failed since most of his people rejected him?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:4</b> - \"Certainly not\" - \"let God be true and every man a liar\"</li></ul>"},{"t":170.6,"lv":3,"h":"3:4 - Psalm 50 (51): David vs God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Quote: \"that you may be justified in your words and may overcome when you are judged\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Psalm 51</b> in English Bibles; <b>Psalm 50</b> in Greek numbering (Orthodox Study Bible)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>David's psalm of repentance</b> after murder and adultery</li><li class=\"d0\">Preceding words: \"the hidden truths of your wisdom you have made clear to me\" / \"against you only have I sinned\"</li><li class=\"d1\">David: you <b>revealed all truth</b> to me - like the Jews with the <b>oracles of God</b> - and I <b>chose to sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"this is <b>one hundred percent my doing</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Image of a <b>court case</b>: when is God judged?</li><li class=\"d1\">OT court cases were <b>civil, not criminal</b> - no law code with penalties; party A says party B wronged them, judge rules who's right</li><li class=\"d1\">David vs God → <b>God is in the right</b>, David in the wrong</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul applies it to <b>the whole nation</b>: given Torah and the rest, they <b>chose to sin</b> → <b>God by no means failed</b></li></ul>"},{"t":371.91,"lv":2,"h":"3:5-6 - Does our sin show God's righteousness?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:5</b> - if our unrighteousness <b>demonstrates</b> God's righteousness, is God unjust to inflict wrath?</li><li class=\"d1\">idea: our sin shows how great, faithful, true God is → why is he upset about sin? \"it brings glory to him\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"pretty <b>sophist</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Calvinist friends</b> assert exactly this: God creates some sinful and wicked, condemns them to hell <b>for his glory</b>, to reveal his wrath and justice</li><li class=\"d1\">\"may sound like sophistry, but there are still people among us who believe this\"</li><li class=\"d1\">argued in the <b>16th century</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:6</b> - \"Certainly not\" - then how will God <b>judge the world</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">if everything just magnifies his glory, <b>how does he restore justice and order</b>? - implies everything already in good order</li><li class=\"d1\">Calvinist view: God the <b>only agent</b>, everything his plan → nothing to correct on the day of the Lord</li><li class=\"d1\">even sinful to question it</li></ul>"},{"t":567.88,"lv":3,"h":"Judgment = restoring order","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul: ridiculous - the <b>very idea of God coming to judge</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>mishpat</i> - judgment in Hebrew Scripture = God <b>sets things right</b>, back in order</li><li class=\"d1\">so they're <b>not as they should be now</b>, not the way he wants</li><li class=\"d0\">God allows disorder <b>for now</b> - patience and mercy, time <b>for repentance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Isaiah: God takes <b>no delight in the death of the wicked</b>; desires none perish but turn and live</li><li class=\"d0\">Not a <b>criminal trial</b> - God restoring correct order → things are <b>out of whack now</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so can't say \"God wants me to sin to display his righteousness\" as excuse</li></ul>"},{"t":687.66,"lv":2,"h":"3:7-8 - \"Let us do evil that good may come\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:7</b> - \"If I lie and that makes you understand God's truth... why am I judged a sinner?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God wanted me to lie to bring glory to him\" → \"I'm <b>doing what God created me to do</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:8</b> - \"Why not say, <b>let us do evil that good may come</b>?\" - as slanderously reported</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>slander against Paul</b> (Acts): telling everyone not to worry about the law, \"it doesn't matter what you do\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul says not only is that false - \"their condemnation is just\" <b>soft-pedals</b> it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"pardon my French\" - basically <b>\"they can go to hell\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he'd just been talking about <b>Christ judging the earth</b> - condemnation is right</li></ul>"},{"t":795.79,"lv":2,"h":"3:9 - All under sin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:9</b> - \"What then? Are we better than they?\" - the ones just said to face final condemnation?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Not at all\" - Jews and Greeks (<b>= Gentiles</b>) charged, <b>all under sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Am I saying it because I think <b>I and you are better</b> than them? - no</li><li class=\"d1\">\"previously charged\" both - ch. 1 (Gentiles) and ch. 2 (Jews)</li></ul>"},{"t":835.17,"lv":3,"h":"\"Under sin\" vs sins","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not \"all <b>committed sins</b>\" - <b>under sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sins / <b>trespasses</b> = a law, a commandment, you break it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sin</b> as power: first in <b>Genesis</b> - Cain and Abel, offering rejected</li><li class=\"d1\">God to Cain: \"<b>sin is crouching at your door</b>\" - like an animal ready to pounce, seeks to <b>master</b> you; you must master it</li><li class=\"d1\">Cain <b>gives in</b>, murders his brother</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's concept: remember <b>\"obedient to unrighteousness\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"at some point you messed up\" - everyone <b>under the power of sin</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Common problem</b> for the unity of the church in Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">not Gentiles idolatrous sinners and <b>Jews good and holy</b> - \"No, both\"</li></ul>"},{"t":981.09,"lv":2,"h":"3:10-18 - Chain of Scripture quotes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">A <b>series of psalm verses</b> - not one continuous psalm; a bit of <b>Ecclesiastes</b> too</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"As it is written\"</b> - weight lost on us</li><li class=\"d1\">we jot notes, type into phones</li><li class=\"d1\">then, writing was a big deal: a copy of <b>Romans</b> would cost ~<b>$5,000</b> in today's money</li><li class=\"d1\">no printing press, no photocopiers; scrap paper = <b>pottery shards</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>\"scriptures\"</b> = \"things written down\" (from Latin) - enshrined, permanent, vs oral tradition</li><li class=\"d1\">\"as it is written\" = <b>appeal to authority</b>, important and true</li></ul>"},{"t":1129.27,"lv":3,"h":"The quoted verses: a state of being","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Read: \"none righteous, no not one... none who seeks after God... throat an open tomb... poison of asps... feet swift to shed blood... <b>no fear of God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not \"the crimes I charge each of you with\" - describing <b>a state of being</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>life under the control of sin</b> - \"a horrible state\"</li><li class=\"d0\">So the solution isn't \"how to get off the hook for guilt\" - it's <b>how you're set free</b></li><li class=\"d1\">free to <b>seek after good, after God</b>; free of poison in your mouth, violence, all the sins of ch. 1</li><li class=\"d1\">things that <b>took possession</b> of you - \"an oppression upon you\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Salvation = <b>from this state</b></li><li class=\"d1\">condemnation = <b>remaining in this state forever</b> by choice - also a picture of <b>wrath</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1288.18,"lv":2,"h":"3:19-20 - The law brings knowledge of sin","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:19</b> - what the law says, says to those under it: <b>every mouth stopped</b>, all the world guilty before God</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:20</b> - by deeds of the law <b>no flesh justified</b>; by the law is <b>knowledge of sin</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"made righteous\" = <b>not</b> how you get to the right side at judgment</li><li class=\"d1\">sheep and goats - <b>you don't become a sheep by keeping the law</b>, \"that's not what it's for\"</li><li class=\"d1\">law reveals everyone's on the <b>condemned side</b> - word \"guilty\" not best</li><li class=\"d1\">law also contains <b>a means for managing</b> that</li></ul>"},{"t":1386.15,"lv":3,"h":"Day of Atonement: sin management","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God living in the midst of his people - <b>tabernacle</b> in the middle of the camp</li><li class=\"d1\">problem: God is holy, <b>a consuming fire</b>; people <b>aren't so holy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">either God <b>breaks out and consumes</b> them, or has to <b>leave</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Day of Atonement</b> yearly (more in <b>Hebrews</b>) - <b>two goats</b>, no sheep</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ as <i>Lamb of God</i> = <b>Passover</b>, not atonement</li><li class=\"d1\">goat 1: high priest places <b>all the people's sins</b> on it; not sacrificed (unclean) - driven into wilderness = <b>scapegoat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">goat 2: <b>sacrificed</b>, blood into the <b>most holy place</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>kipper</i> / <i>kippur</i> = <b>to wipe</b>: sins gone, but they left <b>a stain</b> on the camp</li><li class=\"d1\">cf. \"cursed is the ground because of you\"; Leviticus: sins taint the land, land <b>vomits you out</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so two elements: <b>sins removed</b>, damage <b>purified</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Only <b>manages</b> it - yearly, daily <b>sin offerings</b>; priest offers for his own sins too</li><li class=\"d1\">\"reveals sin and then manages it... until <b>Christ</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Torah = <b>sin management system</b> so God can dwell with them until he dwells with them in Christ and <b>takes care of it once and for all</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1647.01,"lv":3,"h":"Torah never meant to justify","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">So it was <b>never salvation</b> - Christ's death, resurrection, ascension <b>frees us from sin</b>, moves us to the <b>righteous column</b> (sheep from goats)</li><li class=\"d1\">\"The Torah never claimed to do that\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not justified by Torah <b>not</b> because no one kept it perfectly</li><li class=\"d1\">keeping it perfectly includes the <b>sacrifices</b> - doesn't mean never sinning</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Jeroboam</b>: God offers David's covenant \"if you, like David, keep every one of my laws\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Q: Didn't God know <b>Psalm 50</b>? A: <b>David repented</b>, used the prescribed means → <b>kept the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: <b>blameless</b> as to Torah as a Pharisee - \"not being facetious\"</li><li class=\"d0\">But it <b>manages</b> sin: \"a <b>band-aid</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">doctor treating fever and headache, <b>sinus infection</b> remains - function, not cure</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Christ cures</b> - \"Christ was the plan all along\"; Torah <b>prepares a people to produce Christ</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1857.66,"lv":2,"h":"3:21-22 - But now: righteousness apart from the law","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:21</b> - \"But now\" - the righteousness of God <b>apart from the law</b>, witnessed by <b>the law and the prophets</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what God did in Christ is not part of the law - the law and prophets <b>bore witness</b>; <b>fulfillment</b> of what they aimed at</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:22</b> - righteousness of God <b>through faith in Jesus Christ</b> to all who believe</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone has <b>same problem</b> (under sin) → <b>same solution</b>, Jew or Greek</li><li class=\"d1\">believe = faith = <b>faithfulness, loyalty</b> to Christ - includes believing the gospel true, then <b>beyond</b> true/false</li></ul>"},{"t":1950.29,"lv":2,"h":"3:23-24 - All have sinned; justified by grace","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:23</b> - no difference; <b>all have sinned</b> and fall short of the glory of God</li><li class=\"d1\">packed verse - Paul will <b>unpack</b> it, as in ch. 1</li><li class=\"d1\">now <b>committed sins</b> - not only under sin; \"glory of God\" does <b>not</b> come from our sinning</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:24</b> - justified <b>freely by his grace</b> through the redemption in Christ Jesus</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>grace</b> = the <b>action of God</b> - God did it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Redemption</b> (\"redeem a coupon\") = slavery, <i>manumission</i></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ <b>purchased you out</b> of slavery to sin</li><li class=\"d1\">ancient slavery = a <b>debt</b> you couldn't pay; someone pays → free</li><li class=\"d0\">Sin enslaves: first <b>choices</b>, after a while <b>not choosing</b> - addiction: drink, drug; anger \"running you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">righteous not by keeping Torah but by <b>God's action</b>, who <b>bought us back</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2176.36,"lv":2,"h":"3:25 - <i>Hilasterion</i>: atoning sacrifice","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:25</b> - \"whom God set forth as a <b>propitiation</b> by his blood\" (NKJV left as is)</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek <i>hilasterion</i> = OT word for the <b>cover of the Ark</b> - \"<b>mercy seat</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">what was <b>purified on the Day of Atonement</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so \"<b>atoning sacrifice</b>\" better than propitiation; Paul <b>parallels Christ to the Day of Atonement</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2239.89,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: propitiation and the pagan sense","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Propitiation sounds like <b>God's anger appeased</b> - pagan use</li><li class=\"d0\">A: Luther and Calvin followers base it on the <b>pagan use</b>, not the Greek OT and related Hebrew words</li><li class=\"d1\">pagan: angry god/spirit, offer animal's blood to <b>appease</b>, stop it killing you</li><li class=\"d0\">Problems - most obvious: Paul <b>just quoted Psalm 50</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ends: \"you do not delight in <b>burnt offerings</b>... a contrite and humble heart you will not despise\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God doesn't work that way; Paul wouldn't quote it then \"compare God to <b>Zeus</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Clearly the <b>Day of Atonement</b> - Christ as atoning sacrifice</li></ul>"},{"t":2404.41,"lv":3,"h":"Purified, not recategorized","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Christ's blood <b>washes away the taint</b> left by sins - <b>completely, permanently</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not God moving us from one category to another with <b>no change</b> - contra <b>Lutheran and Calvinist friends</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>actually purified</b> by Christ's blood</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Justified\"</b> contains the idea</li><li class=\"d1\">Aramaic word in <b>Daniel</b>: after the temple trampled by <b>Antiochus Epiphanes</b> (Maccabean revolt), the temple will be justified</li><li class=\"d1\">= desecration undone, <b>cleansed, purified</b> - <b>Hanukkah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greek might not carry it → Paul adds <b>mercy seat</b> imagery: high priest takes blood, wipes it</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ the means for Jew and Gentile: moved <b>and purified</b> → no longer under sin, can <b>seek God</b> and be rewarded with eternal life</li><li class=\"d1\">\"God gives it to us in the first place and then he rewards it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2587.27,"lv":2,"h":"3:25-26 - Demonstrating his righteousness","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Why? \"to <b>demonstrate his righteousness</b>\" - justice</li><li class=\"d1\">in the past <b>passed over sins</b> - forbearance with Gentiles; with Israel gave Torah to <b>manage</b></li><li class=\"d1\">done for \"X thousands of years\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:26</b> - now he shows he is just: <b>solves the problem</b>, not just bears with it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"just and the <b>justifier</b> of the one who has faith in Jesus\"</li><li class=\"d1\">justifies those <b>loyal to Christ</b> - purifying, forgiving, cleansing</li></ul>"},{"t":2706.38,"lv":2,"h":"3:27-28 - Boasting excluded","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:27</b> - \"Where is boasting? <b>Excluded</b>\" - by the law of works? No, by the <b>law of faith</b></li><li class=\"d1\">all under sin, enslaved; Christ set us free</li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>Israel leaving Egypt</b> - they did nothing; no \"follow Torah perfectly and I'll free you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God redeems, brings out, <b>then gives the Torah</b> for another purpose</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Deuteronomy 9</b>: \"not because of your righteousness... you are a <b>stiff-necked people</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:28</b> - justified by faith <b>apart from the deeds of the law</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not works in general - Christ judges by works - but <b>works of the law</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2832.73,"lv":3,"h":"Works of the law - circumcision, kosher","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">For his audience: <b>circumcision, keeping kosher</b>, food laws</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Acts 15</b>: only certain commandments applied to Gentiles - back to <b>Leviticus</b> (those for Gentiles living among you)</li><li class=\"d1\">not food laws or circumcision - <b>given only to Israel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God never told Israel to enforce them on <b>Hittites</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Naaman the Syrian</b>: \"worship only Yahweh\" - not told to be circumcised, keep kosher, celebrate Passover</li><li class=\"d1\">only <b>no other God</b>, no sexual immorality, <b>live a moral life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Works of the law = part of the <b>sin management system</b> and <b>Israel's identity</b> - nothing to do with the <b>last day</b></li><li class=\"d1\">uncircumcised and righteous - fine; circumcised and condemned - possible; never eat pork but wicked - condemned</li><li class=\"d0\">So by <b>faith, faithfulness, loyalty to Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>chapter 4</b>: it was <b>always</b> that way - back to <b>Abraham</b>; believing God's promises</li><li class=\"d1\">Torah <b>added later</b> for another purpose</li></ul>"},{"t":3068.82,"lv":2,"h":"3:29-31 - One God; establishing the law","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:29-30</b> - God of the Jews only? <b>Also of the Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">to Jewish audience: he made heavens and earth, <b>scattered the nations at Babel</b>; \"the things they worship aren't gods\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>one God</b> justifies circumcised <b>by</b> faith and uncircumcised <b>through</b> faith - circumcision has nothing to do with it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>3:31</b> - do we make void the law through faith? \"<b>Certainly not</b>... we <b>establish</b> the law\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we make its <b>true purpose clear</b> so it serves its intended function</li><li class=\"d1\">\"which wasn't to make people righteous. It never was.\"</li></ul>"}]});
